<% /* The heavy per-host reference: matcher and event names, the output
   channel, workdir attribution, session-lifecycle events, and timeout
   behavior all differ per host. Each varying region is a whole-block variant
   keyed on one host axis; everything between the blocks renders identically.
   */
const host = it.variant({
  "claude-code": "claude-code",
  codex: "codex",
  opencode: "opencode",
  antigravity: "antigravity"
}); %># Understanding hook output

The touch pipeline runs after supported reads and writes. Static planning runs
before supported shell calls to capture bounded pre-state when needed.

<% if (host === "claude-code") { %>`PostToolUse` observes `Read|Edit|Write|Bash` and injects bounded
`additionalContext` after the tool completes.
<% } else if (host === "codex") { %>`PostToolUse` observes `apply_patch|exec_command|exec|Bash` and injects
bounded `additionalContext` after the tool completes.
<% } else if (host === "antigravity") { %>`PreToolUse` stashes `run_command` input on disk. `PostToolUse` joins on the
step index, gates on the host's `error` field, and queues rendered touch
context for delivery after the invocation's tool batch as an ephemeral
message. Dedicated file tools have no pinned contract and receive no touch
attribution.
<% } else { %>`tool.execute.before` captures shell and patch plans. `tool.execute.after`
observes `bash`/`read`/`edit`/`write`, plus `apply_patch` on gpt models, and
appends bounded `<git-span>` context to the tool output. The experimental
`execute` tool is outside this coverage.
<% } %>
## The touch hook: the merged `<git-span>` block

When an edit lands (or a read touches a partial range) inside a span anchor,
<% if (host === "opencode") { %>the touch hook appends a merged `<git-span>` block to the tool result's
output: a header line, one full span section per surfaced span (sections
separated by `---`), and a single footer after a final `---`. A healthy span
renders as:
<% } else if (host === "antigravity") { %>the touch pipeline renders a merged `<git-span>` block and delivers it after
the invocation's tool batch as one ephemeral message: a header line, one full
span section per surfaced span (sections separated by `---`), and a single
footer after a final `---`. A healthy span renders as:
<% } else { %>the hook injects a merged `<git-span>` block as `additionalContext`: a header
line, one full span section per surfaced span (sections separated by `---`),
and a single footer after a final `---`. A healthy span renders as:
<% } %>
```
<git-span>
checkout.tsx has implicit couplings:

## billing/checkout-request-flow
├─ web/checkout.tsx #L88-L120
└─ api/charge.ts    #L30-L76

Checkout request flow that carries a charge attempt from the browser to the
Stripe-backed server.

---

If you change checkout.tsx check the other files to confirm they still work
together.
</git-span>
```

When the touch leaves genuine content drift behind, drifted anchors carry a
lowercase status suffix and the header and footer switch:

```
<git-span>
This edit put an implicit coupling out of date:

## billing/checkout-request-flow
├─ web/checkout.tsx #L88-L120 — changed
└─ api/charge.ts    #L30-L76

Checkout request flow that carries a charge attempt from the browser to the
Stripe-backed server.

---

Restore agreement before committing. Follow confirmed authority. Preserve
anchor shape; if an address changed, swap the old anchor for the new one
with `git span replace`. Update or retire the why only if its meaning
changed. Require `git span drift billing/checkout-request-flow` to report
zero, then check the other anchors. Conform a side only when confirmed authority
or a satisfied gate decides it; report ambiguity or an obsolete coupling.
</git-span>
```

Each `## <name>` section renders the span's full declared anchor list —
including anchors in files other than the touched one — as a box-drawing
tree grouped by shared path prefix, each leaf's range column showing
`#Lstart-Lend`, followed by the span's why sentence when one is recorded. A
directory holding a single entry folds onto that entry's line, so a branch
only ever appears where two or more anchors actually share a prefix. A
whole-file anchor is a bare path with no range column; where the same file
also carries line ranges, it takes `(whole file)` in the range column so its
own drift label can never be read as belonging to a neighboring range. Only genuine
(semantic or terminal) drift earns a suffix (` — changed`, ` — deleted`, …); positional
drift never does — see below. The header scales with what drifted: `<file>
has implicit couplings:` (naming the touched file) when nothing did, the
singular form above for one drifted span on a write, and `This edit put
implicit couplings out of date:` for more than one. A read never edited
anything — it only surfaces drift that was already there — so its drifted
header names the coupling instead of the touch: `This file has an implicit
coupling out of date:` (singular) or `This file has implicit couplings
out of date:` (plural). With several drifted spans, apply the footer to each
span and use `git span drift <name>` for the final zero-drift check. The block carries
everything needed to act — anchors, statuses, and the description — so no
follow-up `git span` read is required.

### Verified relocations are refreshed before delivery

Before delivery, the hook runs `git span context --fix` for the touched file
or range. It refreshes verified exact-content relocations in selected spans and
elsewhere in addressed files; response selection stays scoped.
Whitespace edits and fuzzy matches remain drifting for review. Repairs update
`.span/` worktree files; include them with the source change when committing.


### When a span surfaces (and resurfaces)

A span renders when its evidence revision differs from the latest one delivered
for that repository and span in this session. The revision covers its definition
and relevant file state. Identical repeats are suppressed; a different change
renders again even with the same status. Returning to an earlier revision after
another change also renders again. Every render includes the full span section.

### What never produces a block

<% if (host === "opencode") { %>- Whole-file `read` (no `offset`/`limit`).
- `write` to a path that doesn't yet exist on disk.
- A `write` that's a full-content replacement (no common prefix/suffix with
<% } else if (host === "antigravity") { %>- Any dedicated file read/edit/write tool call — unpinned tool names mean
  only `run_command` activity is attributed at all.
- A shell write to a path that doesn't yet exist on disk.
- A shell write that's a full-content replacement (no common prefix/suffix with
<% } else { %>- Whole-file `Read` (no `offset`/`limit`).
- `Write` to a path that doesn't yet exist on disk.
- A `Write` that's a full-content replacement (no common prefix/suffix with
<% } %>  what's on disk).
- Whole-file anchors (no `#L…` range) — excluded from intersection matching.
- Gitignored or non-repo files.
- A span whose repository and evidence revision match its latest delivery
  this session.

## Bash intent attribution

Bash and shell calls are attributed from explicit command intent. The
<% if (host === "opencode") { %>`tool.execute.before` planner and `tool.execute.after` handler share one
bounded parser for
<% } else { %>`PreToolUse` planner and `PostToolUse` handler share one bounded parser for
<% } %>literal shell writes and reads, literal-list loops, literal `sed`/Perl
substitutions, and proven literal Python/Node file-edit dataflows. The parser
never runs an embedded program or expands a dynamic path. Unsupported syntax,
globs, command substitutions, arbitrary generators, history-changing Git
operations, and other unproven dataflows produce logger-only unresolved reason
codes and no touch.

Every candidate is scoped to the effective working directory's repository and
filtered through one batched tracked-file query. Files outside the repository,
<% if (host === "opencode") { %>ignored files, span metadata, and untracked files are dropped. The `bash`
tool's declared `workdir` argument is used for both planning and
post-tool attribution, so an explicit working directory inside a different
repository is handled in that repository.
<% } else if (host === "antigravity") { %>ignored files, span metadata, and untracked files are dropped. The
`run_command` call's `Cwd` argument is used for both planning and post-tool
attribution, so an explicit working directory inside a different repository
is handled in that repository.
<% } else { %>ignored files, span metadata, and untracked files are dropped. A Codex
`exec_command` or code-mode `exec` envelope's `workdir` is used for both
planning and post-tool attribution, so an explicit working directory inside a
different repository is handled in that repository.
<% } %>
Most commands need no persisted pre-state. When a delete, substitution range,
or pre-command EOF cannot be reconstructed safely after execution,
`static-plan.mjs` stores one content-minimal record under the session's
`planned-touches/` directory. A record contains only tracked
repository-relative paths, bounded ranges, operation/index metadata, and small
verification evidence; it never contains a file body or repository tree.
<% if (host === "opencode") { %>`tool.execute.after` atomically consumes the record once, verifies the evidence,
<% } else { %>`PostToolUse` atomically consumes the record once, verifies the evidence,
<% } %>reparses ordinary operations, and applies shell join and post-state gates before
touching a span. Success, failure, interruption, and duplicate delivery cannot
<% if (host === "opencode") { %>reuse a plan. Under OpenCode, `session.status` (idle) prunes the session's call-scoped
plans between turns, and `session.deleted` — plus a final `dispose()` sweep at
shutdown — eagerly retire the session memo and any remaining plans;
opportunistic 30-day cleanup covers crashed sessions.

After user input, when any note exists in the repository, `session.status` (idle) sends root sessions one synthetic prompt to
dispatch a forked subagent using <%= it.skillRef('git-span:review-notes') %>.
Synthetic prompts and child sessions do not trigger another review. OpenCode V2 resumes the session through `session.synthetic`.

A command that merely exits nonzero still reaches the after hook under
OpenCode — the bash tool returns with a numeric `exit`, and exit-gated join
semantics apply (a short-circuited `&&` attributes nothing because its write
never ran) — but like the twins' failed commands it can surface a write only
when the parse provides decisive post-state evidence, such as an expected
replacement result; an inconclusive write goes silent under a nonzero exit
rather than firing as an existence-gated advisory. The adapter ignores host-level failure results
(invalid arguments, denied permission, spawn errors). Such a call surfaces
nothing, so even one carrying decisive
post-state evidence, such as an expected replacement result, goes
unattributed. This is the degraded-parity caveat documented in
`references/install-and-trust.md`. Interrupted calls (an `exit: null`
result suppresses like an interruption) stay silent. Response-derived reads
remain a separate pass and share only the session memo, so a later read
can surface context without duplicating command-derived output.
<% } else if (host === "antigravity") { %>reuse a plan. Under Antigravity, an idle, successful model `Stop` requests
a forked subagent using <%= it.skillRef('git-span:review-notes') %> once per
conversation when any note exists in the repository. Empty stops preserve the review guard. Later stops prune the session's call-scoped plans and stashes;
error and step-limit stops do not request review. The surfaced-span memo is deliberately left to its TTL
sweep (pruning it at a turn boundary would make identical spans re-inject
every turn), and opportunistic 30-day cleanup covers crashed sessions.

Antigravity's `PostToolUse` carries no tool response — success collapses to
the absence of the host's `error` field. A command whose input carries
`error` routes to failure attribution and can never surface a write (there is
no post-state evidence to verify against), and response-derived read
attribution is unavailable entirely — the degraded-parity caveat one notch
past OpenCode's. Interrupted calls stay silent.
<% } else { %>reuse a plan. Shell hooks record observed Git commits as session notes;
editing a file alone creates no commit note. Keep the CLI and plugin on the
same release: receipt coordination uses the CLI's native lock helper.
A commit in a repository created or first entered in the same Bash call (`git init`
then `git commit`, or a repository named only through a shell variable) gets no note,
because enrollment observes only the hook's cwd and literal paths.
When notes for the session exist, `Stop` asks the agent to dispatch a forked subagent to load
<%= it.skillRef('git-span:review-notes') %> and review the notes that exist for the session. Stops without notes for the session proceed silently, including when the notes lookup fails.
Stop continuations proceed without another review request. `SessionEnd` on
Claude and empty stops or stop continuations on Codex retire the session memo and plans;
opportunistic 30-day cleanup covers crashed sessions.

A failed Bash command can still surface a write only when the supported family
provides decisive post-state evidence, such as an expected replacement result.
Inconclusive writes and interrupted calls stay silent. Response-derived reads
remain a separate pass and share only the session memo, so a later read can
surface context without duplicating command-derived output.
<% } %>
The parser's candidate ceiling is 32 and is all-or-nothing: a bounded set is
attributed completely or rejected before any touch. Planned records allow at
most 32 touches and 32 ranges per touch, 16 KiB of verification evidence, and
64 KiB total. These are internal safety limits rather than runtime
configuration. The former `GIT_SPAN_SNAPSHOT_*` environment variables and
`git-span.snapshot-*` Git configuration keys have been removed and no longer
affect hook behavior.

Diagnostics are written through the hook logger: resolved read/write counts,
unresolved reason counts, scope/tracked/execution drops, parser and touch
latency, subprocess count, and whether dependency context surfaced. Unresolved
static intent never creates a transcript warning by itself; the safe result is
simply no attribution.

## Failure behavior

The touch pipeline fails open: a missing `git span` binary, timeout, failed
scan, or malformed CLI result injects nothing and does not block the tool.
Unsupported intent, untracked paths, and writes outside the effective
repository remain silent. The tool's working directory selects the repository
for both planning and attribution.

<% if (host === "opencode") { %>OpenCode runs hooks in-process without per-hook timeouts, so the plugin
bounds its own `git span` subprocesses. Host-level failures never reach the
after hook and receive no attribution; see `references/install-and-trust.md`.
<% } else if (host === "antigravity") { %>Antigravity gives hooks no exit-code channel. Every pre-tool handler returns
an explicit `{"decision": "allow"}` because an empty response means denial.
Dedicated file tools receive no touch attribution; see
`references/install-and-trust.md`. Hook timeouts use seconds.
<% } else if (host === "codex") { %>Codex hook timeouts use seconds; Claude Code uses milliseconds. Convert units
when comparing their hook definitions.
<% } %>
Run explicit `git span drift` checks or use CI to verify edits outside the
supported touch events; see `references/ci-and-sync.md`.
